Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** for adding a hillshade layer that is driven by a **raster‑DEM** source to a MapLibre GL JS map.  
Everything is plain JavaScript (no build tools required) and works with MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0 (the current stable line).

---

## 1. Prepare a DEM tile service

MapLibre expects the DEM to be served as **XYZ raster tiles** (or a single GeoTIFF via `url` + `type: "raster-dem"`).  
Typical public sources:

| Provider | URL template | Notes |
|----------|--------------|-------|
| **MapLibre demo tiles** | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | 256 px PNG, EPSG:3857, 0‑255 elevation (scaled by 256 m per unit) |
| **OpenMapTiles Terrain** | `https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Same format as the demo tiles |
| **Your own MBTiles / Cloud‑Optimized GeoTIFF** | `https://myserver.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Must be CORS‑enabled if hosted on another origin |

> **Important:** The DEM tiles must be **encoded as “mapbox”** (the default for the demo tiles) – i.e. each pixel value is an unsigned 16‑bit integer where the actual elevation = `pixelValue * 256`. If you use a different encoding you’ll need to tell MapLibre about it (see the `encoding` property below).

---

## 2. Add the raster‑DEM source to the style

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map (basic boilerplate)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// 2️⃣  When the style is ready, add the DEM source
map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.addSource('my-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    // URL template for the DEM tiles
    url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    // Optional but recommended:
    tileSize: 256,                // size of each raster tile (most services use 256)
    // If your DEM uses a different scaling factor, set it here:
    // encoding: 'terrarium'   // for Mapbox “terrarium” format (elevation = (R*256 + G + B/256) - 32768)
    // encoding: 'mapbox'      // default – pixel value * 256 = meters
    // maxzoom: 14,             // limit the highest zoom you have tiles for
    // attribution: '© OpenMapTiles' // optional attribution string
  });
});
```

### What the `type: "raster-dem"` does

* It tells MapLibre that the raster tiles contain **elevation data**, not imagery.  
* The engine will internally decode the pixel values into **meters above sea level** (using the `encoding` you specify).  
* Once the source exists you can use it for **hillshade**, **terrain 3‑D**, or **custom shaders**.

---

## 3. Add a **hillshade** layer that consumes the DEM source

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // … add the DEM source first (see above) …

  // 3️⃣  Add the hillshade layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-hillshade',
    type: 'hillshade',
    source: 'my-dem',          // <-- the raster‑DEM source we just added
    // No "source-layer" is needed because raster sources have no layers
    paint: {
      // Optional visual tweaks (all are numbers in the range 0‑1 or colors)
      'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315, // degrees clockwise from north
      'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,           // 1 = real‑world slope, >1 = steeper
      'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
      'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
      'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777'     // optional, for mid‑tone shading
    }
  });
});
```

#### Paint properties you can play with

| Property | Meaning | Typical range / values |
|----------|---------|------------------------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | Sun azimuth (0 = north, clockwise) | 0‑360 (default 315) |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | How “steep” the terrain looks | 0‑5 (default 0.5) |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | Color of the shadowed side | any CSS color |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | Color of the illuminated side | any CSS color |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | Mid‑tone accent (optional) | any CSS color |

You can animate any of these (e.g. rotate the sun) by calling `map.setPaintProperty('my-hillshade', ...)` inside a `requestAnimationFrame` loop.

---

## 4. (Optional) Enable **3‑D terrain** on the same DEM

If you also want a 3‑D terrain surface (extruded terrain mesh) you can add a `terrain` property to the map’s style:

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // … DEM source already added …

  // 4️⃣  Enable terrain rendering
  map.setTerrain({
    source: 'my-dem',
    exaggeration: 1.5   // optional, makes mountains look higher
  });
});
```

> **Note:** The hillshade layer will continue to work **independently** of the terrain mesh. You can keep both, or hide the hillshade (`visibility: 'none'`) if you only want the 3‑D surface.

---

## 5. Full working example (copy‑paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster‑DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,          // tilt the view so hillshade is visible
    bearing: -20
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'mapbox'   // default, kept for clarity
    });

    // Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': '#2b2b2b',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': '#e0e0e0',
        'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777'
      }
    });

    // (optional) 3‑D terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser and you’ll see a sloped, shaded terrain with a subtle 3‑D extrusion.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears (just a blank map)** | DEM source not reachable (CORS, 404) | Open dev‑tools → Network → verify the DEM tiles load. Add proper CORS headers or host on the same origin. |
| **Hillshade looks flat / all one colour** | Wrong `encoding` (e.g., using `terrarium` data with default `mapbox` decoder) | Set `encoding: 'terrarium'` (or the correct value) when adding the source. |
| **Terracotta‑coloured “tofu” squares** | The DEM tiles are not raster‑DEM (they’re imagery) | Use a true elevation raster (PNG/WEBP with elevation encoded) or switch the source `type` to `"raster"` and use a separate hillshade service. |
| **CORS error when loading DEM tiles from another domain** | Server missing `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header | Enable CORS on the tile server, or proxy the tiles through a same‑origin endpoint. |
| **Hillshade disappears after zooming past maxzoom** | DEM tiles not available at that zoom level | Set `maxzoom` on the source to the highest zoom you have, or provide higher‑resolution tiles. |
| **Shadows look too dark / too light** | `hillshade-exaggeration` or sun direction not appropriate for your area | Tweak `hillshade-exaggeration` (0‑2) and `hillshade-illumination-direction` (0‑360). |

---

## 7. Going further

| Goal | How to achieve it |
|------|-------------------|
| **Dynamic sun movement** | In a `requestAnimationFrame` loop, compute a new azimuth (`(Date.now()/1000) % 360`) and call `map.setPaintProperty('my-hillshade', 'hillshade-illumination-direction', azimuth)`. |
| **Custom colour scheme** | Use CSS colour strings or `rgba()` for the four hillshade paint properties. |
| **Combine with vector labels** | Keep your base style’s `text-font`/`glyphs` configuration untouched; hillshade works independently of vector label rendering. |
| **Offline usage** | Download the DEM tiles (or a single GeoTIFF) and serve them from a local HTTP server; the same `raster-dem` source works as long as CORS is satisfied. |
| **Higher‑resolution DEM** | Use a source that serves 512‑px tiles (`tileSize: 512`) or a Cloud‑Optimized GeoTIFF (`type: "raster-dem", url: "my.tif"`). |

---

### TL;DR checklist

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (URL template, `tileSize`, optional `encoding`).  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** that points at that source; tweak paint properties as needed.  
3. (Optional) **Enable 3‑D terrain** with `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: …})`.  
4. Verify CORS, tile availability, and correct `encoding`.  

That’s all you need to get a beautiful, shaded relief map in MapLibre GL JS! 🚀
